SRC/eff_make -- one file replaced for this collection=====================================================utime_osk.c (2026-09-18), and utime.c is gone.WHY.  make has to be able to say "this target is now newer than itssources" -- that is what `make -t' does -- and on Unix that is utime().OS-9's C library has none, so every port of make carries one.  The one thismake came with was headed    /*     * Copyright (c) 1988 by Michael Hoffmann, Muenchen     */and nothing else: a copyright with no grant of any kind.  The rule thiscollection works to (SOURCES.txt, and rdoggett 2026-09-11 on exactly thisfile) is that such a file stays out of the build, so it is out, and this isthe collection's own.WHAT IT DOES.  Opens the file, reads its descriptor with _gs_gfd, writes thefive date bytes OS-9 keeps there -- year less 1900, month, day, hour, minute-- and writes the descriptor back with _ss_pfd.  A directory has to beopened with the directory bit in the mode or the open is refused, so thefile is stat'ed first.  There are no seconds in an OS-9 date, so a timerounds down to the minute, and the access time has nowhere to go.There is one way to do that job, so it resembles every other utime() writtenfor OS-9.  It does not, however, share the fault of the one it replaces,which built the five bytes with    sprintf ( buf.fd_date, "%c%c%c%c%c", ... )-- five characters and a terminating NUL into a five-byte field, so everytouch also wrote a zero over fd_link, the file's link count.MEASURED, 2026-09-18: `make' builds a target and then leaves it alone; `make-t' prints `touch out' and the target's timestamp moves (19:52 to 19:54across a touch, watched over a real minute, since OS-9 dates haveminute resolution).  tools/datatests/dev.cases has both as cases -- andnote the makefile there names `cat' in full, because datatest sets no PATHand the shell make forks cannot find a bare one.